menu "Bathroom Knob"

    menu "Display"

        choice KNOB_RENDER_MODE
            prompt "LVGL render mode"
            default KNOB_RENDER_PARTIAL
            help
                How frames are drawn and sent to the panel. The hardware test
                measures each to pick the smoothest with the least tearing.

            config KNOB_RENDER_PARTIAL
                bool "Partial: two 40-line buffers in internal RAM"
            config KNOB_RENDER_FULL
                bool "Full: two full frames in PSRAM, whole screen redrawn"
            config KNOB_RENDER_DIRECT
                bool "Direct: two full frames in PSRAM, only changes redrawn, whole frame sent"
        endchoice

        config KNOB_LCD_PCLK_MHZ
            int "QSPI clock (MHz)"
            range 10 80
            default 80
            help
                80 MHz measured stable on this panel (26 Oct 2026).

    endmenu

    menu "Power"

        config KNOB_LIGHT_SLEEP
            bool "Stop the processor while the screen is off"
            default y
            help
                Automatic light sleep whenever the dial is asleep. Without it
                the processor keeps running at its lowest clock around the
                clock, which measured 19% of the battery an hour on 3 Nov 2026
                - sleeping saved almost nothing.

                The USB console is not given up for it: IDF holds light sleep
                off while a USB host is actually plugged in, so bench work is
                unaffected and only a cable-free dial on the wall sleeps. A
                console left watching a sleeping dial goes quiet until it is
                woken.

        config KNOB_DEEP_SLEEP
            bool "Drop to deep sleep after a long spell untouched"
            default y
            help
                CORE_DEEP_SLEEP_MS after the last touch or detent, the dial powers
                almost everything down. It comes back on a turn of the knob - a
                tap cannot wake it, because the touch chip is read over I2C and
                nothing is running to read it - and takes a few seconds, since
                Wi-Fi, Music Assistant and the covers all have to come back.

                It wakes itself on a timer every CORE_DEEP_SLEEP_CHECK_MS as
                well, behind a dark screen, to report its charge and ask whether
                a new firmware is waiting: a dial in deep sleep is off the
                network, so without that an update could never land unattended.

                Both timings live in core/core.h with every other timing in
                this project; this only decides whether it happens at all. Turn
                it off for bench work where a dial that disappears off the
                network mid-test is a nuisance.

    endmenu

    config KNOB_BENCH_ON_BOOT
        bool "Run the scripted performance test at boot"
        default n
        help
            It can also be started at any time by typing "bench" on the USB serial port.

endmenu
